Subject: [PATCH] media: software HEVC decoding on linux-arm64 and windows-x64

The ffmpeg configurations for linux-arm64 and windows-x64 build the HEVC
decoder and parser, matching linux-x64. On those builds canPlayType,
MediaSource.isTypeSupported and mediaCapabilities.decodingInfo report
HEVC Main and Main10 as supported, as real Chrome does on the claimed
macOS and Windows machines. A GPU-less host has no hardware HEVC
decoder, so without the software one these calls contradict the claimed
platform. The arm64 build links ffmpeg's own NEON HEVC routines.

diff --git a/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py b/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
--- a/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
+++ b/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
@@ -995,7 +995,14 @@
         '--enable-parser=aac,h264',
     ])
 
-    if target_os == 'linux' and target_arch == 'x64':
+    # Every target whose checked-in config carries the software HEVC decoder.
+    # Keep this in step with the per-target stanzas in ffmpeg_generated.gni: a
+    # regeneration that omitted a target here would silently produce a config
+    # that contradicts the one in the tree. 'linux-noasm' is deliberately absent
+    # because it builds without assembly, and 'mac' because VideoToolbox already
+    # answers for HEVC on every Apple host.
+    if (target_os, target_arch) in (('linux', 'x64'), ('linux', 'arm64'),
+                                    ('win', 'x64')):
         configure_flags['Chrome'].extend([
             '--enable-decoder=hevc', '--enable-parser=hevc',
         ])
